# 3) Data model

Start with a simple survey model.

```ts
type Survey = {
  id: string;
  clientId: string;     // local UUID
  name: string;
  location: {
    lat: number;
    lng: number;
  };
  notes: string;
  status: "draft" | "submitted";
  version: number;      // incremented on server changes
  updatedAt: string;
  createdAt: string;
  deletedAt?: string | null;
};
```

For sync, keep an **outbox queue** of local mutations.

```ts
type OutboxItem = {
  id: string;
  entityType: "survey";
  entityId: string;
  operation: "create" | "update" | "delete";
  payload: any;
  baseVersion?: number;
  createdAt: string;
  retries: number;
  lastError?: string;
};
```

---

# 4) App architecture

Use **local-first reads** and **queued writes**:

## Read flow
- UI reads surveys from local DB only
- Never depend on network to render core screens

## Write flow
- Write to local DB immediately
- Add a mutation to outbox
- UI updates instantly
- Background sync attempts to push changes when online

## Sync flow
- On reconnect, send queued mutations to server
- Server validates version/conflicts
- Server returns updated canonical record
- Client updates local DB and removes processed queue items

