Below is a minimal working embedded-style sensor-processing pipeline in C that reads accelerometer samples, computes a simple feature, and flags anomalies when the signal deviates too much from a running baseline.

It is intentionally small and suitable as a starting point for MCU firmware.

## What it does
- Takes raw accelerometer readings `(ax, ay, az)`
- Computes acceleration magnitude
- Maintains a running mean and variance using Welford’s algorithm
- Flags an anomaly if the current magnitude is more than `threshold_sigma` standard deviations from the baseline

---

## Minimal C implementation

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

typedef struct {
    float mean;
    float m2;
    unsigned int count;
} RunningStats;

typedef struct {
    RunningStats stats;
    float threshold_sigma;
    bool initialized;
} AnomalyDetector;

void stats_init(RunningStats *s) {
    s->mean = 0.0f;
    s->m2 = 0.0f;
    s->count = 0;
}

void stats_update(RunningStats *s, float x) {
    s->count++;
    float delta = x - s->mean;
    s->mean += delta / s->count;
    float delta2 = x - s->mean;
    s->m2 += delta * delta2;
}

float stats_variance(const RunningStats *s) {
    if (s->count < 2) return 0.0f;
    return s->m2 / (s->count - 1);
}

float stats_stddev(const RunningStats *s) {
    return sqrtf(stats_variance(s));
}

void detector_init(AnomalyDetector *d, float threshold_sigma) {
    stats_init(&d->stats);
