Below is a solid high-performance **async web server** example in **Rust** using **Tokio + Hyper**. It’s built to handle many concurrent connections efficiently with non-blocking I/O.

### `Cargo.toml`
```toml
[package]
name = "high_perf_web_server"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

[dependencies]
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }
hyper = { version = "1", features = ["server", "http1", "http2"] }
hyper-util = { version = "0.1", features = ["server", "tokio"] }
http-body-util = "0.1"
bytes = "1"
```

### `src/main.rs`
```rust
use bytes::Bytes;
use http_body_util::Full;
use hyper::body::Incoming;
use hyper::service::service_fn;
use hyper::{Request, Response, StatusCode};
use hyper_util::rt::TokioIo;
use tokio::net::TcpListener;

type RespBody = Full<Bytes>;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let addr = "0.0.0.0:8080";
    let listener = TcpListener::bind(addr).await.expect("bind failed");
    println!("Listening on http://{}", addr);

    loop {
        let (stream, peer_addr) = match listener.accept().await {
            Ok(v) => v,
            Err(e) => {
                eprintln!("accept error: {}", e);
                continue;
            }
        };

        tokio::spawn(async move {
            let io = TokioIo::new(stream);

            let service = service_fn(handle_request);

            if let Err(err) = hyper::server::conn::http1::Builder::new()
                .keep_alive(true)
                .serve_connection(io, service)
                .await
            {
                eprintln!("error serving {}: {}", peer_addr, err);
            }
        });
    }
}

async fn handle_request(req: Request<Incoming>) -> Result<Response<RespBody>, hyper::Error> {
    match (req.method(), req.uri().path()) {
        (&hyper::Method::GET, "/") => Ok(Response::new(Full::from(Bytes::from_static(
            b"Hello from a high-performance Rust web server!",
        )))),

        (&hyper::Method::GET, "/health") => Ok(Response::new(Full::from(Bytes::from_static(b"OK")))),

        _ => {
            let mut not_found = Response::new(Full::from(Bytes::from_static(b"Not Found")));
            *not_found.status_mut() = StatusCode::NOT_FOUND;
            Ok(not_found)
        }
    }
}
```

### Why this is high-performance
- **Non-blocking async I/O** with Tokio
- **Connection reuse** via keep-alive
- **Lightweight concurrency** using `tokio::spawn`
- **No per-request threads**, so it scales well under load

